= Compiler =

== Overview ==
A '''compiler''' transforms a source representation, usually a program, into a target representation while preserving intended behavior to the degree required by the language and implementation.

Targets can include machine code, bytecode, another programming language, an intermediate representation, hardware description, or other executable/analyzable form.

== Front end ==
A compiler front end commonly performs:

* lexical analysis;
* parsing;
* semantic analysis;
* type checking;
* construction of intermediate representations.

These stages map source text into structured program representations.

== Middle end ==
Compiler middle ends analyze and transform intermediate representations.

Optimizations can improve runtime, code size, energy, memory traffic, or other properties.

Optimization is common but not required for compilation.

== Back end ==
The back end maps intermediate representations toward a target architecture or runtime.

Tasks can include instruction selection, register allocation, scheduling, and code emission.

== Compiler and interpreter ==
A compiler translates a program before or during execution.

An interpreter directly executes or evaluates a representation.

Modern systems can combine both, for example bytecode compilation followed by interpretation and just-in-time compilation.

Therefore:

<pre>
compiler ≠ interpreter
</pre>

== Correctness ==
Compiler correctness asks whether translation preserves source-language semantics under the intended conditions.

Compiler bugs can silently change program behavior, so testing and formal verification matter.

